@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Variant comparing an INTERNAL-BDD view (internal parts of
'                 multiple block definitions + cross-block item flows) against
'                 a single-block IBD view (the interior of ONE block).
'   usecase:      Teach the distinction: internal-BDD shows inter-block
'                 internal structure; IBD zooms one block's interior.
'   complexity:   medium
'   domain:       sysml internal block definition / view variant
' ============================================================================
title Example Internal BDD - Internal BDD vs IBD

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Mapping note
'   Two views of the same system are shown side by side.
'   LEFT  = INTERNAL-BDD: internals of TWO block definitions (TankBlock,
'           PumpBlock) plus the item flow crossing their boundary.
'   RIGHT = IBD: the interior of a SINGLE composite block (PumpStation)
'           containing the same parts as nested part usages, with one
'           enclosing boundary.
'   The internal-BDD is more abstract — it shows how the blocks' internal
'   parts and ports relate ACROSS block boundaries. The IBD zooms into one
'   block and treats the others as part usages inside it.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
note over "TankBlock", "PumpStation (IBD)"
  LEFT (internal-BDD): two block definitions,
  each with its own parts and ports, joined by
  a cross-block item flow.
  RIGHT (IBD): one enclosing block owns both
  parts as part-usages; the cross-block flow
  becomes an internal connector.
end note

' ===================== LEFT: INTERNAL-BDD VIEW ============================
class "TankBlock" <<block>> {
  +out : FluidPort        '<<flow>>
  part supply : FluidStore
}

class "PumpBlock" <<block>> {
  +in : FluidPort         '<<flow>>
  part intake : IntakeChamber
  part impeller : Impeller
}

' Cross-boundary item flow between two distinct block definitions.
"TankBlock" -(#darkblue)->[color=#darkblue] "PumpBlock" : FluidFlow { rate, pressure }

' ===================== RIGHT: IBD VIEW ====================================
class "PumpStation (IBD)" <<block>> {
  +tankOut : FluidPort    '<<flow>> (delegated)
  +stationIn : FluidPort  '<<flow>> (delegated)
  part : TankBlock        'as tank
  part : PumpBlock        'as pump
}

' Inside the IBD the cross-block flow is an internal connector between two
' part-usages under one enclosing boundary.
"PumpStation (IBD)" -->[color=#darkblue] "PumpStation (IBD)" : <tank.out> FluidFlow { rate, pressure }

note right of "PumpStation (IBD)"
  ONE block boundary. The tank and pump are
  PART USAGES inside PumpStation. Outer ports
  delegate to the parts. The former
  cross-block flow is now an internal
  connector. This is what a single-block IBD
  shows; the internal-BDD on the left is the
  broader, multi-block definition-level view.
end note

@enduml
